  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 935 AM MDT Wed Jun 10 2026\n\nA Heat Advisory is in effect for the Rio Grande Valley in El Paso\nand Hudspeth Counties until 9PM MDT tomorrow night. Temperatures \nwill run up to 7 degrees above normal for the lowlands today and \ntomorrow due to the upper-level ridge stretching from Arizona to \nthe Gulf Coast. Dry winds will prevail from the west today and \ntomorrow for the CWA, lowering relative humidity percentages for \nthe lowlands down to the lower teens/upper single digits, removing\nany chance of showers or thunderstorms.\n\nAs it stands, the ridge on Friday is forecast to start drifting \nto the east, allowing for some moisture to return to the \nBorderland, bringing temperatures back to the upper 90s for the \nweekend as well an isolated (10-30%) chance of afternoon showers \nor thunderstorms. The best chance of a thunderstorm on Friday is \nup in the Sacramento Mountains. Moisture will stay in the region\nthrough the weekend and into early next week, continuing chances\nfor showers and thunderstoms each afternoon. However, in the \nscenario that the ridge should stay in place and strengthen, more \ntriple digit temperatures will be expected for the weekend.\n \nFor now, I am sticking to the \"drifting ridge\" scenario, but an \nadjustment to the forecast may be needed if the \"anchored-in- \nplace\" scenario plays out. For the start of next week, a \nMidwestern-based trough will push the upper- level ridge west of \nour area, so we might not get as hot early next week as we did \nthis week. It remains to be seen though, since extended model runs\nindicate the ridge strengthening back over our area for the end \nof next week, and if these extended model runs play out, we could \nbe back to 100 degree temperatures by the end of next week.",
